<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stavanger</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stavanger is a city in Rogaland on the south-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. It grew around its cathedral, begun around 1125 when the town became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat, and later lived from fishing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canning">canning</a> and shipping. Since the 1970s it has been the centre of Norway's offshore oil industry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hafrsfjord">Battle of Hafrsfjord</a> was fought nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stavanger.jpg" alt="Stavanger" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ryan Hodnett · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Breiavatnet_-_Stavanger,_Norway_2021-08-01_(02).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Stavanger is a city and municipality on the Stavanger Peninsula in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and the administrative centre of Rogaland county. It lies in a coastal landscape bordering the sea to the west and Boknafjorden to the northeast, with the Byfjorden and Gandsfjorden along its east side. It is part of Low-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaeren">Jæren</a>, a flat area of marsh, sand and stone, and most of the municipality lies between 0 and 50 metres above sea level. Its climate is temperate oceanic and very mild by Nordic standards. The Old Norse form of the name was Stafangr; the most used interpretation is that it first named the inlet now called Vågen, the original site of the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first traces of settlement come from about 10,000 years ago, when the ice retreated after the last ice age. Rich archaeological material suggests that chiefs in North Jæren held considerable power from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and some historians argue that the area was an economic and military centre in the 9th and 10th centuries. Stavanger emerged in the 12th century, a time of population growth and urbanisation across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and grew into a centre of church administration and a market town of the south-west coast. Written and archaeological sources on its first development are sparse. Its official founding year is counted as 1125, when the cathedral was completed.</p><p>In the early 20th century its industry was tied to fisheries and shipping. It was known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canning">canning</a>, and in the 1950s more than 50 canneries operated there, so that the town was called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s canned capital; the last of the factories closed in 2002. On 8 April 1940, before the German invasion, the captain of the Æger sank the German freighter Roda by his own decision after customs officials grew suspicious of its cargo. In 1969 oil was discovered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, and Stavanger was chosen as the on-shore centre for the industry on the Norwegian sector. Statoil, now Equinor, was founded in 1972, and a period of hectic growth followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Old Stavanger has a collection of 18th- and 19th-century wooden houses that are protected. Stavanger Cathedral, dedicated to St Swithun, was built between 1100 and 1150 by the English bishop Reinald in Anglo-Norman style, with a Gothic choir added in the late 13th century; it is the only Norwegian cathedral almost unchanged since the 14th century. The city is the seat of the University of Stavanger, established as a university in 2004, and of the Norwegian Petroleum Museum. Stavanger and its region were a European Capital of Culture in 2008, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stavanger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its founding is traditionally credited to Harald Fairhair, who according to the sagas defeated a coalition of rival kings at the Battle of Hafrsfjord near modern <b>Stavanger</b>, an event conventionally dated around 872 but placed by many historians somewhat later, perhaps in the 880s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rogaland, around modern <b>Stavanger</b>, was home to a powerful Iron Age elite.</blockquote><a href="/state/rogaland">Rogaland</a></li></ul></section>
